      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 508,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a transparent, odorless, and tasteless liquid that is essential for all known forms of life. \n\nTo truly understand what water is, we can look at it through a few different lenses: its chemistry, its unique physical properties, its role on Earth, and its importance to life.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Chemistry of Water (What it's made of)\nChemically, water is a molecule. Its chemical formula is **$H_2O$**. \nThis means every single molecule of water is made up of:\n*   **Two Hydrogen atoms**\n*   **One Oxygen atom**\n\nThese atoms are held together by strong covalent bonds. Because oxygen attracts electrons more strongly than hydrogen, water is a **polar molecule**. This means the oxygen side has a slight negative charge, and the hydrogen side has a slight positive charge. This polarity is the secret behind many of water’s amazing properties.\n\n### 2. The Three States of Matter\nWater is one of the few substances on Earth that naturally exists in three distinct forms depending on the temperature:\n*   **Liquid:** The standard form we drink, swim in, and find in rivers and oceans.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Formed when water freezes at or below 0°C (32°F). \n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Formed when water boils at 100°C (212°F), though it also evaporates into the air at lower temperatures.\n\n### 3. Unique and Magical Properties\nWater behaves differently than almost any other substance on Earth. Because of its molecular structure, water has several unique traits:\n*   **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because it is polar, water can dissolve more substances (like salt, sugar, and minerals) than any other liquid. This is how our bodies transport nutrients and how oceans stay salty.\n*   **Ice Floats:** Almost every liquid shrinks and gets denser when it freezes. Water actually *expands* and becomes less dense. That is why ice cubes float in your glass—and why ice floats on top of lakes, allowing fish to survive underneath during the winter.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This is why the oceans help regulate the climate of the entire planet, and why your body doesn't boil over when you exercise"
